{
  "enabled": true,
  "name": "Auto-Commit Hook",
  "description": "Automatically creates semantic version commits when tasks are completed in spec files or when tests pass, following conventional commit format and semantic versioning principles",
  "version": "1",
  "when": {
    "type": "fileEdited",
    "patterns": [
      "**/.kiro/specs/*/tasks.md",
      "**/test/**/*.test.js",
      "**/test/**/*.test.ts",
      "**/tests/**/*.test.js",
      "**/tests/**/*.test.ts",
      "**/__tests__/**/*.js",
      "**/__tests__/**/*.ts",
      "**/*.spec.js",
      "**/*.spec.ts"
    ]
  },
  "then": {
    "type": "askAgent",
    "prompt": "A file has been modified that may require an automatic git commit. Please analyze the changes and create appropriate commits following these guidelines:\n\n## Analysis Instructions\n\n1. **Determine File Type**: Check if the modified file is a tasks.md file or a test file\n2. **For tasks.md files**: Look for tasks that have been marked as completed (status changed to completed)\n3. **For test files**: Run the tests to ensure they pass before creating any commits\n\n## Task Analysis (for tasks.md files)\n\nWhen tasks are completed:\n- Identify the spec name from the file path (e.g., 'auto-commit-hook' from '.kiro/specs/auto-commit-hook/tasks.md')\n- Analyze the completed task content to determine the type of change:\n  - **feat**: New features, enhancements, major functionality additions\n  - **fix**: Bug fixes, corrections, problem resolution\n  - **refactor**: Code restructuring, optimization, cleanup\n  - **docs**: Documentation updates, README changes\n  - **test**: Test additions, test improvements\n  - **chore**: Maintenance tasks, configuration changes\n\n## Test Analysis (for test files)\n\nWhen test files are modified:\n- Run the appropriate test command for the file type and location\n- Only proceed with commit if ALL tests pass\n- If tests fail, do not create any commits and log the failure\n\n## Semantic Versioning Rules\n\nDetermine version increment based on change impact:\n- **MAJOR** (x.0.0): Breaking changes, major architectural changes, API breaking changes\n- **MINOR** (0.x.0): New features, enhancements, new capabilities, non-breaking additions\n- **PATCH** (0.0.x): Bug fixes, refactoring, documentation, test improvements, minor changes\n\n## Version Management\n\n1. Check if package.json exists and has a version field\n2. If no version exists, initialize with '0.1.0'\n3. Read current version and increment according to the rules above\n4. Update the version in package.json\n\n## Commit Message Format\n\nUse conventional commit format:\n```\n<type>(<scope>): <description>\n\n[optional body]\n```\n\nWhere:\n- **type**: feat, fix, refactor, docs, test, chore\n- **scope**: spec name (e.g., 'auto-commit-hook') or test suite name\n- **description**: Brief description of what was completed\n\nExamples:\n- `feat(auto-commit-hook): implement task completion detection`\n- `test(voice-verification): add identity verification test cases`\n- `fix(memory-agent): resolve state persistence issue`\n\n## Git Operations (Gitflow Structure)\n\n1. **Repository Check**: Verify this is a git repository and gitflow is initialized\n2. **Branch Management**: Follow gitflow branching strategy:\n   - **Feature branches**: For new features (feat commits) - create/use `feature/*` branches\n   - **Hotfix branches**: For critical fixes (fix commits) - create/use `hotfix/*` branches\n   - **Release branches**: For version releases - create/use `release/*` branches\n   - **Development branch**: Merge completed features into `develop` branch\n   - **Main branch**: Only for production releases from `release/*` or `hotfix/*` branches\n3. **Branch Operations**:\n   - If on `main` or `develop`: Create appropriate feature/hotfix branch based on commit type\n   - If already on feature/hotfix branch: Commit directly to current branch\n   - Use branch naming: `feature/spec-name-task-description` or `hotfix/issue-description`\n4. **Stage Changes**: Stage all modified files related to the change\n5. **Create Commit**: Use the generated commit message following conventional commits\n6. **Gitflow Integration**: \n   - For completed features: Optionally merge to `develop` if all tasks in spec are complete\n   - For hotfixes: Prepare for merge to both `main` and `develop`\n   - For releases: Update version and prepare release branch\n7. **Error Handling**: If git operations fail, log the error but don't block the workflow\n\n## Error Handling\n\n- If not a git repository: Log warning and skip commit\n- If gitflow not initialized: Initialize gitflow with default settings or log warning\n- If no staged changes: Stage all modified files before committing\n- If version file missing: Initialize package.json with version 0.1.0\n- If tests fail: Skip commit and log test failure details\n- If git commands fail: Log error details and continue\n- If branch creation fails: Fall back to committing on current branch\n- If merge conflicts occur: Log conflict details and require manual resolution\n\n## Multiple Changes\n\nIf multiple tasks are completed or multiple test files modified simultaneously:\n- Combine into a single commit with the highest impact level for versioning\n- List all changes in the commit message body\n- Use the most significant change type for the commit type\n- Create appropriate gitflow branch based on the most significant change type\n- If mixing features and fixes, prioritize hotfix branch creation for critical fixes\n\nPlease analyze the current file changes and execute the appropriate commit workflow based on these instructions."
  }
}